            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Mandarin for in all, then the Mandarin for to count."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: 打扫",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "打扫 — dǎsǎo — \"to clean (a room); to sweep\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "今天我们一起打扫 jīntiān wǒmen yìqǐ dǎsǎo — Today we're cleaning up together."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The new character, in pieces: 扫 (sǎo) — the hand piece on the left, as in 打, and on the right three strokes: a bar that turns down, and two bars inside it. The next lesson puts it in your hand."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Two third tones: dǎsǎo is said dásǎo, the first rising."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "打扫 (dǎsǎo) is built from 打 'to strike', here 'to do', and sǎo 'to sweep', both inherited from Middle Chinese, taengX and sawX (Guangyun, in Baxter's transcription). The regular sound change from taengX would not give the everyday dǎ; why is not settled."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for everyday actions."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
